[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16, mswitch0, router17, sshswitch
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the core link-aggregation (bonding) between the routers and the “switch” appliances, which is directly impacting OSPF.

Mechanism: Both core interconnects are running Linux bonding in round-robin mode (balance-rr). This transmits successive frames on alternating links, which is known to reorder and duplicate control packets. OSPF hellos/DBDs/LSUs arriving out of order intermittently force neighbor state resets and extra SPFs across the area, presenting to users as brief campus-wide “freezes” followed by recovery.

Localization: The unstable adjacency is observed across the core. On router16 and mswitch0, and on router17 and sshswitch, bond0 is configured for round-robin: cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0 shows “Bonding Mode: load balancing (round-robin)” on all four nodes. OSPF corroborates recent churn: show ip ospf on routers reports multiple recent SPFs (15–18) and neighbor detail shows several state changes per adjacency, despite no physical errors or drops on interfaces.

Root cause: Core interconnects use balance-rr bonding (on router16<->mswitch0 and router17<->sshswitch), which reorders OSPF control traffic and intermittently resets adjacencies.

Corrective fix: Convert the core bonds to a deterministic mode on both ends—prefer 802.3ad (LACP) with matching xmit_hash_policy layer3+4, or simply use active-backup (mode=1) if LACP isn’t available. As an immediate stabilization step, disable one slave on each bond to run single-link, which will stop reordering and halt OSPF flaps without restarting FRR. Once bonding is corrected symmetrically on both sides, re-enable aggregation; OSPF should then remain steady. 
=== END ===